Only for some values of "most efficient."  It's the most efficient use
of _time_ or _turns_, most likely, but I'm not certain that it is the
most efficient use of a _wish_.

Consider SDSM: is wishing for SDSM the most efficient way of acquiring
SDSM?  Maybe, in the early game, but once the hero can handle 4-6
silver dragons and has sufficient* luck,  the most efficient (in terms
of wish use) sequence is to wish for a magic marker, write a charging
scroll, write a genocide scroll, write an enchant armor scroll, and
reverse genocide the dragons.  Presto! +1 SDSM and a magic marker with
charges remaining.

* - sufficient to write the potentially unknown genocide scroll
without using too many charges from the magic marker.

I didn't think that I was breaking new ground here with this
question.  I'm simply hypothesizing that the average number of magic
marker charges needed to give the hero teleport control might be less
than that available from a wished for magic marker.

... okay I looked at some numbers.  To keep it simpler I only looked
at the 50 charges available in the magic marker following
recharging.

parameters:
50 charges;
15 - 29 charges per scroll of genocide;
4-6 tengu per scroll;
50% chance of a corpse;
1/6 chance of acquiring TC;

worst: 29 charges ~ 1 scroll ~ 4 tengu ~ 2 corpses ~ 31% chance of
acquiring TC
average: 22 charges ~ 2 scrolls ~ 10 tengu ~ 5 corpses ~ 60% chance of
acquiring TC
best: 15 charges ~ 3 scrolls ~ 18 tengu ~ 9 corpses ~ 81% chance of
acquiring TC

Now, I'm not saying that this is the best way to go,
or that my math is correct,
I'm just saying that it seems this gamble could payoff and instead of
using an entire wish on an extrinsic, one could take a risk and end up
with two intrinsics and a partially used magic marker.



